---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout id="pipeline" title="Pipeline">

  <h1>Pipeline</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Since the Crusoe already receives native VLIW molecules that have already been translated
    and scheduled by the CMS, it makes the pipeline hardware fairly simple.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when a molecule enters the front of the pipeline its atoms are easily routed to the
    appropriate execution unit because the CMS already did the work. 
    In a conventional x86 processor, the hardware has to dynamically determine this making
    the hardware much more complex.
  </p>


  <h4>
    The pipeline contains 5 total execution units:
  </h4>

  <ul>
  <li>2x ALU</li>
  <li>1x Load/Store</li>
  <li>1x FP</li>
  <li>1x Branch</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The reason there are only 4 atoms per molecule if we have 5 execution units is because 
    the type of operation that each slot in the molecule is fixed to a certian group of operations.
    One of the atoms is a "Compute" atom which means it can either be another ALU or FP operation.
  </p>


  <img
    src="/images/crusoe-pipeline.png"
    alt="Transmeta Crusoe pipeline showing the ALU, load/store, floating-point, and branch pipelines"
    class="figure"
  />




  <h3>Fetch0, Fetch1, Decode</h3>

  <p>
    Although these stages are drawn for each type of unit, they are mostly shared 
    beforehand with 
    much
    of the work being done by the way 
    the molecule is formatted. After these three stages, each unit enters its own execution path.
  </p>

  <h3>Commit</h3>

  <p>
    This is the stage where an operation has the option to commit which will be explained in further
    detail in the Precise Exceptions section. In short this is where, at the end of a translation unit
    the work would be commited into the shadow registers as safe "checkpoint".
  </p>


  <h2>Multiple Molecules in the pipeline</h2>

  <p>
    Different operations have different pipeline latencies, so CMS 
    schedules dependent operations to occur when their operands are 
    expected to be available. Crusoe has very few hardware interlocks. 
    Unpredictably long operations, such as loads that miss in the cache, 
    are handled automatically by the hardware; Transmeta does not describe
     this mechanism in much detail.

  </p>


</Layout>